The Mercedes-Benz A210 Evolution was part of the first generation A-Class (W168), produced from 2002 to 2004. It featured a 2.1L inline-4 petrol engine producing 138 hp and 151 lb-ft of torque. Acceleration from 0-60 mph was around 7.7 to 8.6 seconds depending on transmission and body style (standard or long wheelbase). Top speed was around 126-127 mph. It was front-wheel drive and available with a 5-speed manual or 5-speed automatic transmission.

Fuel economy was around 28-30 US mpg combined. CO2 emissions were around 190-202 g/km. As a petrol car, it has no electrification features.

Dimensions varied slightly between the standard (SWB) and long-wheelbase (LWB) versions. Length was around 3640 mm (SWB) or 3810 mm (LWB), width was 1719 mm, and height was around 1587-1601 mm. Wheelbase was 2423 mm (SWB) or 2593 mm (LWB). Cargo space was around 390 liters with seats up. It has 5 seats. Ground clearance was around 11.3 cm (4.45 inches). Turning circle was around 10.75-11.35 meters (35.27-37.24 ft).

Reliability of the W168 generation was mixed, with some owners reporting issues, though others had good experiences. Common concerns included suspension, rust, and electronics. Maintenance costs can be higher due to it being a Mercedes. Used prices in the UK range from around £1,000 to £7,000+. Insurance groups are around 20-24A.

The W168 A-Class received a 5-star Euro NCAP safety rating. ADAS features were non-existent for its era. Visibility was generally good due to the tall design. Headlights were basic halogen. Security features were standard for the time.

Infotainment was very basic, typically a radio/CD player with no modern screen or smartphone connectivity. USB ports were not a feature. Comfort and convenience features were minimal compared to modern cars.

The A210 Evolution had AMG styling elements and sport suspension, making it the top performance trim of the W168. It carries the Mercedes-Benz brand prestige, but as the entry-level model of its time, it sits lower in the model hierarchy. The interior was basic Mercedes for its era, with minimal tech focus. The style was tall and somewhat boxy compared to modern cars.

